window.docContents[3].resolve({"/Appendix/#section-chapter-5-maps":{"contents":"* pathNat₁Translate: embedding of the infinite path pathNat₁ into itself.* Embedding.blowUp: canonical embedding of a graph G into its blow-up.* hypercube.embedBool / hypercube.embedFalse / hypercube.embedTrue: embeddings\n  of Q[n] into Q[n+1].* Embedding.mycielskianLeft: embedding of a graph G into its Mycielskian mμ[G].* Hom.mapMycielskian: graph homomorphism mμ[G] →g mμ[H] induced by a homomorphism\n  G →g H.* Embedding.mapMycielskian: graph embedding mμ[G] ↪g mμ[H] induced by an\n  embedding G ↪g H.* Iso.mapMycielskian: graph isomorphism mμ[G] ≃g mμ[H] induced by an isomorphism\n  G ≃g H.* tensorProd / G ⊗g H: tensor (categorical) product of two graphs, on the vertex\n  type V × W.* tensorProd.fstHom / tensorProd.sndHom: the two projections out of the tensor\n  product as graph homomorphisms.* tensorProd.prod: the homomorphism into tensorProd G H induced by a pair of\n  homomorphisms (universal property of the product).* piTensorProd / ⨂g i, G i: tensor product of an arbitrary family of graphs.* IsVertexTransitive: predicate for a graph being vertex-transitive.* cycleGraphRotate: automorphism of C[n] rotating the vertices modulo n,\n  sending d to 0.* HypercubeVert.xor / ⊕ₕ: bitwise xor addition on hypercube vertices.* hypercubeTranslate: automorphism of Q[n] by translating by a vertex.* isoMycielskianCompleteTwoCycleGraphFive: the isomorphism mμ[K[2]] ≃g C[5].* IndFree: A.IndFree B means that B contains no induced copy of A.* ClawFree: a graph is claw-free if it contains no induced copy of the claw\n  K[1, 3].* lineGraphCompleteGraphThreeIso: the isomorphism L[K[3]] ≃g K[3].* lineGraphClawIso: the isomorphism L[K[1, 3]] ≃g K[3].\n\nFrom the exercises:\n\n* pathGraphEmbedding: embedding of a path graph P[n] into a longer path\n  P[n + k].* Embedding.mapBlowUp: embedding of G into the blow-up H.blowUpGraph W given\n  G ↪g H and nonempty parts.* hypercubeEmbedding: embedding of Q[n] into Q[n + k] by iterating\n  hypercube.embedFalse.* hypercubeTwoIsoCycleFour: the Gray-code isomorphism Q[2] ≃g C[4].\n\n","context":"Graph Theory in Lean\u0009Appendix\u0009Definitions","header":"14.2.4. Chapter 5: Maps","id":"/Appendix/#section-chapter-5-maps"}});